From 2a30f63a34c4b78e4a40e71bc7a3cb016f7ba5e1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Trilochan <trilohere@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 13:18:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Add a guided demo: real collisions and seven stories the
 system tells itself

The control centre answers "how well does this perform" and the street view
is a sandbox to poke at. Neither answers the question an audience actually
has, which is "what is this thing doing and why should I care". That needs a
story with a beginning and an end.

New page at /demo. Pick one of seven stories and it stages that event in the
live simulation: a crash and everything that follows, a pedestrian seen
around a corner, arriving on green, a liar being found out, a cloud outage,
the roadside units learning together, or all of it at once.

Down the side is a numbered checklist of what the story claims will happen.
Each line latches, with the tick it happened on, only when the running
simulation genuinely does it. Nothing is animated: a step the system does
not produce stays dark. That constraint is the whole design -- a scripted
animation that always succeeds would prove nothing.

Collisions are now modelled rather than approximated by dropping an
obstruction. Two vehicles on the same road are wrecked, go immobile, block
the lane and keep announcing the accident on a duty cycle (DENM causeCode 2,
confidence 1.0 -- the sender is the accident). One event then chains four
modules: the traffic behind is warned before it can see anything, peers
corroborate the report into a confirmed incident, an ambulance is dispatched,
and the corridor asks junctions for priority. Where no road has two vehicles
on it the second is brought in, and the result says `staged: true` rather
than pretending traffic did it.

Several things were measured rather than assumed, and the measurements
changed the design:

Declared story lengths were guesses and all of them were wrong -- the crash
cascade completes in 3 ticks, not the 55 I had written. Progress is now shown
against beats completed rather than ticks elapsed, because a tick bar sitting
at 5% on a story that has already finished says the opposite of the truth.
The clock also stops when a story ends, since otherwise the scene the
checklist describes has scrolled away before anyone can look at it.

Certificate revocation genuinely needs 200-320 ticks of evidence. Rather than
lower the threshold to make the demo snappier, the story was given the time
it needs, split so the intermediate trust collapse is visible, and annotated
to say the slowness is deliberate: wrongly revoking an honest vehicle is
worse than tolerating a liar for a while.

Two real defects surfaced. Ambulances were dispatched from a random node, so
roughly one time in sixteen they spawned on the incident with a route of one
node -- no journey, no corridor, nothing to watch; and an origin whose only
signalised junction was under its own wheels asked for no priority at all,
because priority is requested for junctions ahead. Dispatch now prefers a
station whose route passes a signal it has not yet reached. Separately a
wrecked vehicle returned early from its tick, so it announced the accident
but never shared the pedestrian standing in front of it, starving collective
perception on the one road where a stopped car is the only thing with a view.

Steps that legitimately do not fire every run are labelled as such. Signal
priority lands in about four runs in six -- it depends on where the junctions
fall along the route and the ask can be lost -- and a dark line should read
as an honest lossy radio, not a broken demo.

Also: the narration collapsed. On a nine-RSU grid every junction broadcasts
on the same duty cycle, so SPaT was seven lines out of seven and drowned out
everything worth reading; it is now one line per tick.

176 Python tests and 59 TypeScript tests pass; ruff, mypy, tsc and eslint are
clean. Beat coverage was checked headlessly across six seeds before any of it
was wired to a page, and the whole thing was driven in a browser afterwards:
all seven stories offered, the crash story completing 7/7, the standards
detail toggling, no failing requests on any route, and no horizontal overflow
at 400px.

